# What this project does

Playwright automation that logs into the Rush SailPoint ISC sandbox, searches for a
test identity by Stage Key, walks through Identity Details, Access
(Roles/Entitlements — every table page when results are paginated), Accounts,
and per-source Account Detail pages, captures highlighted screenshots at each
step, and compiles everything into a Word (.docx) report per scenario.

# Architecture

- `tests/config/testcases.ts` — the data. `TEST_CASES` (array of scenarios) and
  `SOURCE_FIELD_PROFILES` (per-source extra fields). This is the only file that
  should change when adding a new scenario or a new source-specific field.
- `tests/helpers/run_regression_case.ts` — the actual automation logic
  (`runRegressionCase`), shared by both spec files below. Field lists
  (`DETAIL_FIELDS_BASE`, `ACCOUNT_DETAIL_FIELDS_BASE`) live here.
- `tests/rush_regression.spec.ts` — loops every entry in `TEST_CASES`, runs them
  all back to back.
- `tests/rush_regression_adhoc.spec.ts` — hand-edit four constants at the top
  (scenario name, primary/secondary source + stage key) and run just that one
  case. This is the day-to-day file for testing a single new identity without
  touching `testcases.ts` or triggering every other scenario.
- `tests/helpers/screenshotEvidence.ts` — DOM highlighting/capture primitives.
- `tests/helpers/buildReport.ts` — compiles the .docx report.
- `tests/helpers/sharepointUpload.ts` — automatically uploads the Word report
  to the team SharePoint folder (the real destination). A local staging file is
  written only long enough to upload, then deleted. No drag-and-drop. One-time
  sign-in may be required if the SharePoint session is missing/expired.
  Report names: `{IdentityName}_{yyyy-MM-dd}_{HH-mm-ss}.docx`.

# Field highlighting — how it actually works

`highlightFields(page, labels)` matches **exact visible text** (case-insensitive,
trimmed) against DOM leaf elements. A label that doesn't match on-screen text
**silently does nothing** — no error, the field just isn't highlighted. Always
verify a new field's exact on-screen label (via screenshot + zoom) before adding
it to a field list; don't guess.

It returns `Record<string, string[]>` — an **array** per label, not a single
string. This matters because some labels (e.g. `Status`) appear more than once on
a page (a top-level badge plus a separate attribute-grid field). Collapsing to a
single value silently drops one occurrence. `collectBlanks()` in
`run_regression_case.ts` checks every occurrence individually.

Two different naming conventions exist depending on the page:
- **Identity Details page** (identity-cube level): space-separated, e.g.
  `Correlation Key`, `Employee Type`, `Manager Name`.
- **Account Detail page** (per-source account level): underscore-separated, e.g.
  `Correlation_Key`, `Employee_Type`, `Manager_Name`.
These are related but distinct concepts — don't assume a field confirmed on one
page type exists with the same label on the other.

# Field profile system

- `DETAIL_FIELDS_BASE` / `ACCOUNT_DETAIL_FIELDS_BASE` — highlighted on every
  scenario regardless of source.
- `SOURCE_FIELD_PROFILES[sourceName].detailExtras` /  `.accountDetailExtras` —
  additional fields only highlighted when that specific source is in play.

Known source-specific rules (from the Rush design doc's Appendix A + Lifecycle
tables):
- **Legal Hold / Manager Hold** apply to RUSH Lawson and Copley Lawson (both).
- **Non-Employee Workforce**: Legal Hold applies, Manager Hold explicitly does
  NOT — only `Legal_Hold` is in that source's profile on purpose.
- **ECHO Credentialed Providers** and **Ellucian Students**: neither hold type
  applies — intentionally no hold fields in either profile.
- `Primary_Position` matters for RUSH Lawson and Copley Lawson (multiple possible
  assignments per identity); not meaningfully used elsewhere.

# Identity Profile attribute mapping — important gotchas

Confirmed from Admin > Identity Profile > Mappings (not guessed):

- **Distinguished Name** is sourced from the **TEST RUSH AD** source
  (`distinguishedName` attribute via a Search transform) — NOT from RUSH Lawson
  or any HR source. It shows up on the Identity Details page (identity-cube
  level) but does not exist as a labeled field on any HR source's own Account
  Detail page. `highlightValuesContaining(page, 'OU=')` is used as a fallback
  substring match on Account Detail pages since no confirmed label exists there.

- Several identity-cube attributes (`Employee Type`, `Correlation Key`,
  `Manager Name`, `Birth Date`, `Relationship Status`/Lifecycle State) are
  **hardcoded to pull from RUSH Lawson specifically** in the mapping — not from
  "whichever source is primary" in a given test scenario. This means: **do not**
  build a blind field-by-field comparison between the Details page and every
  source's account — for identities where RUSH Lawson isn't the source in
  question, mismatches would be expected/normal, not errors. The one comparison
  that IS safe and implemented: **Correlation Key**, since every correlated
  account's own `Correlation_Key` should genuinely match the Details page's
  value regardless of source (see "Correlation Key Check" report section).

- The six `Active *` flags (`Active Copley Infor`, `Active NonEmployee`,
  `Active Provider`, `Active Rush Infor`, `Active Student`, `Active Workday`)
  are each independently derived from a different source's `IIQDisabled` via
  its own transform. They all live on the Details page simultaneously
  regardless of primary source, so they're in the shared base, not gated to
  any one source's profile.

# Report sections generated automatically

- **What Was Checked** — auto-generated from the field lists actually used for
  that run (not hand-maintained).
- **Correlation Key Check** — flags real correlation problems (see above).
- **Fields Found Blank** — lists highlighted fields whose value was blank/`--`/
  `-`/`n/a`/`null` at capture time. This is presence-detection only, not a
  business-rule check (e.g. it won't flag "Manager_Hold populated on
  Non-Employee Workforce" as wrong — only that a field was or wasn't blank).

# Known environment gotchas

- `playwright.config.ts` only runs the `chromium` project by default (firefox/
  webkit are commented out) — this was intentional, they were racing on the same
  output file paths since none of the three projects were scoped by testMatch.
- `auth.setup.ts` uses `page.pause()` for manual MFA on a fresh login — this
  **requires `--headed`**. Cached sessions (`playwright/.auth/user.json`, valid
  ~1hr) skip this, so headless is fine once a session exists.
